  "query_preview": "..Current satellite and upper-air analysis show an \nunusually deep low-pressure system off the southern California \ncoast. This system is pulling a rich subtropical moisture plume \ninto the state, yielding record amounts of atmospheric moisture \nfor this time of year. As a result, active weather conditions \ncontinue across northern Arizona tonight, with scattered \nthunderstorms capable of producing heavy rain, along with severe \nhail and powerful downdraft winds. In addition, radar indicates \nthe presence of rotating supercells due to 0-6 km deep-layer bulk\nshear in excess of 50 kts. \n\nThe West Coast low will continue to pump warm, moist low level \nair into the state as it moves inland and lifts to the northeast \ntoday. Unstable conditions (CAPE of 500-1500 J/kg) will combine \nwith 40-55 kts of deep-layer bulk shear to produce additional \nrounds of severe, supercell thunderstorms. These storms will be \ncapable of producing hail between 1 and 2 inches in diameter, \ndamaging winds in excess of 58 mph, and even a few tornados. The \nSPC has reissued a Severe Thunderstorm Watch for northern Arizona,\nwhich is in effect until 10 AM MST. In addition, heavy rain will \naccompany these fast moving storms. So, flash flooding and flooding\nare concerns in areas that receive multiple rounds of convective \nactivity. Soils are saturated across northern Arizona, and flash \nflooding is already occurring across portions of the Navajo \nNation within Moenkopi Wash and Chinle Creek. A Flood Watch \ncontinues through 11 PM MST tonight. The passing low is forecast \nto push a cold front through the region from later this afternoon \nthrough Friday morning, shifting the risk for severe weather and \nflash flooding eastward as it does so. Behind the front, drier \nand more stable conditions will inhibit storm formation. \n\nOn Friday, dry conditions return, with only low chances for\nshowers and thunderstorms (10-25%) forecast over the higher \nterrain of eastern Arizona. That said, a closed low is forecast to\ndevelop over northern Arizona this weekend bringing well below-\nnormal temperatures and an uptick in shower and thunderstorm \nactivity Saturday, perhaps persisting into Sunday. These storms \nwill develop in an environment characterized by low shear, low \ninstability and much less atmospheric moisture, resulting in a \nlow severe weather and flash flood threat. Quiet weather is \nforecast to start next workweek.",
